# {{ project_name }} — working notes for agents

{{ description }}

One bounded domain, one codebase, one database schema.
{% if service_shape == 'both' %}It runs as an API (`app/main.py`) or as a queue
worker (`app/worker.py`) — same image, entrypoint chosen per deployment.
{% elif service_shape == 'worker' %}It runs as a queue worker (`app/worker.py`).
There is no HTTP surface; adding one means adding `main.py` and a `router.py`,
not regenerating.
{% else %}It runs as an API (`app/main.py`). Adding background work means adding
`worker.py` and a `jobs.py`, not regenerating.
{% endif %}
Built on [Keel](https://github.com/francisco-ltech/keel), which supplies the
cache, database, identity{% if has_worker %} and queue{% endif %}. The rules below come from Keel and are not
stylistic — each one exists because the obvious alternative has a failure mode.

## Layout

```
app/modules/<domain>/
  models.py       SQLAlchemy. Never imports schemas.
  schemas.py      Pydantic. Never imports models.
  repository.py   data access; takes a session, never opens one
  service.py      business logic; owns the transaction, returns schemas
{% if has_api %}  router.py       HTTP only; calls services
{% endif %}{% if has_worker %}  jobs.py         job handlers; call the same services
{% endif %}```
{% if has_api %}
`modules/sessions/` has no `models.py` and no `repository.py` on purpose: a
session is a token in Keel's store, not a row here. `app/security.py` holds the
bearer dependency and `app/observability.py` the request-id middleware — both
web-framework vocabulary, so both stay in this project rather than in Keel,
whose core imports no web framework.
{% endif %}
## Rules

**Never write a `get_session` dependency.** A session held across a request hits
an open FastAPI deadlock and starves the pool while p99 still looks healthy.
Routes take services; services open the transaction:

```python
async def approve(invoice_id: UUID) -> InvoiceRead:
    async with uow() as session:
        invoice = await Invoices(session).get_or_fail(invoice_id)
        invoice.approve()
        return InvoiceRead.model_validate(invoice)
```

**Services return schemas, not ORM objects.** A detached instance raises on the
next attribute access. Convert inside the block.

**Eager-load relationships you will use.** Lazy loading raises under async
rather than quietly issuing a query — `Repository.eager(...)`.

**Argon2 goes on a thread and outside `uow()`. Both, every time.** It is ~40 ms
of deliberate CPU. Inline from `async def` it stalls every other request in the
process; inside a transaction it does that while a pooled connection sits idle.
`app/modules/users/service.py` has `hash_in_a_thread`; verifying looks the same:

```python
hasher = password_hasher()
matched, replacement = await anyio.to_thread.run_sync(hasher.verify_and_upgrade, password, stored)
```
{% if has_api %}
**Verify against `None` when the user does not exist. Never return early.**
This is the one that gets written wrong:

```python
async with uow() as session:
    user = await UserRepository(session).get_by_email(email)
    stored = None if user is None else user.hashed_password  # values, not the row

matched, replacement = await anyio.to_thread.run_sync(hasher.verify_and_upgrade, password, stored)
if user is None or not matched:
    raise AuthenticationError(INVALID_CREDENTIALS)
```

`if user is None: raise` above the verify would be shorter and would turn the
endpoint into a user-enumeration oracle: hashing takes tens of milliseconds and
returning early takes none, so a stopwatch answers "does this address have an
account here?" for every address in a list. Passing `None` hashes against a
dummy so the miss costs what a hit costs. The reply has to be uniform too — one
message for a wrong password and an unknown address, and the same number of
queries on the way there.

**Persist the replacement hash when `verify_and_upgrade` returns one.** Signing
in is the only moment the plaintext exists, so it is the only moment a password
hashed at last year's cost can be upgraded. Dropping the value silently leaves
those accounts cheap forever.

**A password change needs the current password, and revokes every session.**
`PUT /users/{user_id}/password`, never a field on `UserUpdate`: a password set
without proving knowledge of the old one is account takeover wearing an update.
After it lands, `token_store().revoke_subject(user_id)` — and the same after
disabling or deleting an account, or the tokens issued before it still work.
{% endif %}
**Authorize in the service, against a resource, never in the router.** Every
service function that acts on a named thing opens with
`authorize("update", Account(user_id))` — before `uow()`, so a refusal opens no
transaction. The rule is in `app/policies.py`, keyed by the resource's class. A
guard at the HTTP edge would protect only the callers who arrive over HTTP.

**A new resource type needs a policy before it needs a route.** An unregistered
one is refused with a `ConfigurationError`, which this application reports as a
500: a missing registration is a wiring bug, and answering 403 would hide it
among ordinary refusals. Nothing is ever permitted because a rule was forgotten.

**A policy takes `Identity | None` and opens by refusing `None`.** There is no
guest object; the annotation is what makes both type checkers stop you reading
`identity.id` before you have said what an unauthenticated caller gets.

**A new ability is a `match` arm, not a new dependency.** The `case _` arm is
the deny-by-default. Do not reach for `getattr(policy, action)` — that is the
same string, resolved dynamically over an object's whole attribute surface.

**An `Identity` is a value, never the `User` row.** It is frozen, serialisable
and safe to put on a context variable or in a job payload; an ORM instance
outlives its session and raises `DetachedInstanceError` somewhere far away.
Build it at the edge from what you already know, and put nothing on it that has
a source of truth elsewhere — `claims={"email": ...}` is a copy of a column in
{% if has_api %}the token store{% else %}every job payload{% endif %}, stale from the first address change.

**Read the caller with `require_identity()`, not from a parameter.** It works
identically in a service, in a job running under `acting_as(...)`, and in an
audit column. `current_identity()` is the form that returns `None`; there is no
guest object, because a guest with an `id` looks like a caller everywhere the
check is forgotten.
{% if has_api %}
**Require a caller with `Caller`, not by checking inside the endpoint.**
`app/security.py` exports two: `Caller` refuses the request without a valid
token, and `MaybeCaller` hands back `None` for endpoints that behave differently
when signed in but do not require it. Both bind `acting_as` for the whole
request, and both must stay `async def` — a sync dependency runs in a
threadpool, where a context variable set is invisible to the endpoint. A route
that changes somebody's account and takes no caller is account takeover; the
annotation is the whole fix.

**A route decides nothing else.** There is no `Owner` dependency any more and
adding one back would be a second mechanism for a job one thing already does.
Whether the caller may act is the service's `authorize` call, above.

**A 401 carries `WWW-Authenticate`.** `handle_authentication_error` in
`app/errors.py` does it; raise `AuthenticationError` and it is handled.

**`AuthorizationDeniedError` is a 403, and it is the one `KeelError` that is
not a 500.** `handle_authorization_denied` in `app/errors.py` is registered for
it specifically; without that it would fall through to `handle_keel_error` and a
refused caller would be paged on as a server fault. Three auth outcomes, three
statuses: 401 no credential, 403 refused, 500 nothing bound.

**`AuthenticationRequiredError` is a 500, not a 401.** Keel raises it when
`require_identity()` finds nothing bound, which means the edge never
authenticated — a wiring bug. Mapping it to 401 tells a caller to retry with the
credential that just worked, and hides the fault in the bucket nobody pages on.
Do not register a handler for it. ADR 0007, section 8.
{% endif %}{% if has_worker %}
**`dispatch()` inside a transaction waits for the commit.** That is the default
and it is correct: pushing immediately means a worker acting on a row that was
rolled back. Do not pass `after_commit=False` to "fix" a timing problem.

**Raise `PermanentFailureError` when a retry cannot help** — a missing row, a
validation error, a 4xx. Otherwise the job burns its attempts and looks flaky
rather than broken.

**Jobs are dataclasses, retried, and may run more than once.** Write handlers to
be idempotent; at-least-once is the only guarantee a queue can make.
{% endif %}
**Never call `logging.basicConfig`.** `configure_logging(settings.logging_config())`
is the entrypoint, called once{% if has_api and has_worker %} by `create_app` and by `app.worker.main`{% endif %} before
anything else. It installs a JSON formatter *and* a log record factory that puts
the ambient fields on every record, so `basicConfig` after it replaces the first
half and silently keeps the second.

**Add a field with `correlate`, never by passing it down.**

```python
from keel.observability import correlate

with correlate(tenant=tenant_id):
    ...
```

It merges with what is already bound, an inner scope may overwrite a name, and
the previous mapping comes back on the way out{% if has_worker %} — including into a job the
block dispatches, since the fields are sealed onto the envelope{% endif %}. Values are
coerced to strings and `None` is dropped.

**A credential-shaped field *name* is refused; no value is ever inspected.**
`password`, `token`, `api_key`, `cookie`, `bearer` and the rest raise at the
`correlate` call. That catches the reflex — `correlate(token=bearer)` in a piece
of middleware — and nothing more: nothing can tell that `reference` holds a
credential, and a secret bound under an innocent name reaches the log like any
other field. So the rule you actually follow is yours, not the mechanism's: a
correlation field reaches every log line{% if has_worker %}, every envelope and
`keel_failed_jobs.context`{% endif %}, so carry an id and never the thing itself. The same
reasoning is why only `Identity.id` reaches a record and `claims` does not.
{% if has_api %}
**The request id comes from `app/observability.py`, and nothing else mints one.**
`RequestContext` is plain ASGI rather than `@app.middleware("http")` on purpose:
`BaseHTTPMiddleware` runs the rest of the application in a separate task, so
what a context variable set there is visible to depends on the Starlette version
installed. Do not "simplify" it into a decorator.

**It also answers the unhandled `500` itself**, because `add_middleware` puts it
*inside* `ServerErrorMiddleware` — whose reply goes out through the outer `send`,
where no header can be stamped. Removing that branch loses the id on the one
response anybody raises a ticket about, and every other test still passes.

**Uvicorn is run with `--no-access-log`**, and `configure_logging` takes the
handlers off `uvicorn`/`uvicorn.error` so its traceback propagates to the root
and comes out as JSON like everything else. Undo either and the stream carries
two shapes.
{% endif %}
**Soft-deleted rows are filtered globally.** You do not add `WHERE deleted_at IS
NULL`. To include them, `with_deleted(...)` explicitly.

**Test with the fakes.** {% if has_worker %}`fake_queue()` records dispatches without running
them and `fake_cache()` does the same for the cache. Asserting a job was
enqueued and testing what the job does are two different tests.{% else %}`fake_cache()` records what was
cached without a Redis behind it.{% endif %}{% if has_api %} `fake_tokens()` is the exception that proves the
rule — it records *and* works, because a test that signs in has to be able to
use the credential it was handed.{% endif %}

**The test suite hashes cheaply on purpose.** `HASHING_*` is turned down in
`tests/conftest.py`, not in `.env.example`. Argon2 is slow by design and that
slowness is the defence; a suite that creates a user per test pays it per test.

**Tests run against real Postgres inside a transaction that is rolled back.**
There is no fake database — the queries are the part most likely to be wrong.

## Commands

`just` lists everything. `just check` is the full gate and what CI runs.
{% if has_worker %}`just worker` runs the worker; `just dev` runs {% if has_api %}both{% else %}it with reload{% endif %}.
{% endif %}
Postgres is on {{ postgres_port }} and Redis on {{ redis_port }}, to avoid
clashing with anything on the default ports. `just up` starts them.

## Style

Inline comments: two lines maximum, clear and brief. Longer explanations belong
in the docstring. Docstrings say *why*, not *what*.

Migrations: `just revision "what changed"`, then read the generated file before
committing it. Autogenerate misses things and invents others.
